 rectangle whose sides lie on grid lines; a tile may be of any integer dimensions.  \nTiles are required to be pairwise disjoint (each unit square is covered by at most one tile).\n\nA tiling must satisfy:\n\n* In every row there is exactly one unit square that is not covered by any tile.\n* In every column there is exactly one unit square that is not covered by any tile.\n\nConsequently the set of uncovered squares forms a *permutation matrix*: there is a bijection \\(\\sigma:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\to\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) such that the uncovered squares are precisely  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,\\sigma(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\nAll other squares are covered.  \nDenote by \\(A\\) the \\(n\\times n\\) binary matrix whose entry \\(a_{ij}=1\\) iff the square \\((i,j)\\) is covered.  \nThen  \n\n\\[\nA = J - I_{\\sigma},\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(J\\) is the all\u2011ones matrix and \\(I_{\\sigma}\\) is the permutation matrix of \\(\\sigma\\) (i.e. \\((I_{\\sigma})_{ij}=1\\) iff \\(j=\\sigma(i)\\)).  \nSince a permutation matrix is obtained from the identity matrix by a simultaneous permutation of rows and columns, it has the same rank as \\(I\\).  \nThus \\(A\\) is similar (by row/column permutations) to  \n\n\\[\nJ-I,\n\\]\n\nso \\(\\operatorname{rank}(A)=\\operatorname{rank}(J-I)\\).\n\n---\n\n### 1. Upper bound \u2013 a tiling with \\(n\\) tiles\n\nChoose the permutation \\(\\sigma(i)=i\\) (the identity).  \nHence the uncovered squares are the diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\).  \n\nFor each row \\(i\\) place a single tile  \n\n\\[\nT_i = \\{(i,j)\\mid j\\neq i\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich is a \\(1\\times (n-1)\\) rectangle covering all squares of row \\(i\\) except the diagonal one.  \nThe tiles \\(T_1,\\dots ,T_n\\) are pairwise disjoint because they occupy distinct rows.  \n\n*Every row* contains exactly one uncovered square \\((i,i)\\) (by construction).  \n*Every column* also contains exactly one uncovered square, namely \\((i,i)\\) in column \\(i\\).  \n\nThus this tiling satisfies all requirements and uses exactly \\(n\\) tiles.  \nHence  \n\n\\[\n\\text{minimum number of tiles}\\le n.\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 2. Lower bound \u2013 rank argument\n\nConsider any admissible tiling.  \nLet the tiles be \\(R_1,\\dots ,R_k\\).  \nFor each tile \\(R_t\\) define its *incidence matrix* \\(M_t\\) by  \n\n\\[\n(M_t)_{ij}= \n\\begin{cases}\n1 & \\text{if } (i,j)\\in R_t,\\\\[2pt]\n0 & \\text{otherwise.}\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nBecause a tile is a rectangle, there exist binary vectors \\(u_t\\in\\{0,1\\}^n\\) (indicator of the rows of \\(R_t\\)) and \\(v_t\\in\\{0,1\\}^n\\) (indicator of the columns of \\(R_t\\)) such that  \n\n\\[\nM_t = u_t\\,v_t^{\\!\\top}.\n\\]\n\nHence each \\(M_t\\) has rank\u202f1 (over the real numbers).  \n\nThe tiles cover each covered square exactly once and never cover an uncovered square, so  \n\n\\[\nA = \\sum_{t=1}^{k} M_t .\n\\]\n\nThe rank of a sum of matrices does not exceed the sum of their ranks; therefore  \n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{rank}(A) \\le \\sum_{t=1}^{k}\\operatorname{rank}(M_t)=k .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nNow compute \\(\\operatorname{rank}(A)\\).  \nSince \\(A\\) is similar to \\(J-I\\), it suffices to find the rank of \\(J-I\\).  \nThe matrix \\(J\\) has eigenvalues \\(n\\) (once) and \\(0\\) (multiplicity \\(n-1\\)).  \nSubtracting \\(I\\) shifts all eigenvalues by \\(-1\\); thus \\(J-I\\) has eigenvalues  \n\n\\[\nn-1\\quad\\text{(multiplicity 1)},\\qquad -1\\quad\\text{(multiplicity }n-1\\text{)} .\n\\]\n\nAll eigenvalues are non\u2011zero, so \\(J-I\\) is invertible and  \n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{rank}(J-I)=n .\n\\]\n\nConsequently \\(\\operatorname{rank}(A)=n\\).  \nInsert this into inequality (1) to obtain  \n\n\\[\nk \\ge n .\n\\]\n\nThus any admissible tiling uses at least \\(n\\) tiles.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Conclusion\n\nWe have exhibited a tiling with exactly \\(n\\) tiles and proved that no tiling can use fewer than \\(n\\) tiles.  \nTherefore the minimal possible number of tiles is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2025}.\n\\]\n\n---\n\n**Re