ered squares are the main diagonal \\((i,i)\\).\n\n*For the left side.*  \nFor each column \\(j\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) place a vertical tile covering the squares  \n\n\\[\n\\{(i,j)\\mid i=j+1,\\dots ,n\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich is a rectangle of size \\((n-j)\\times1\\).\n\n*For the right side.*  \nFor each column \\(j\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) place a vertical tile covering the squares  \n\n\\[\n\\{(i,j)\\mid i=1,\\dots ,j-1\\},\n\\]\n\na rectangle of size \\((j-1)\\times1\\).\n\nThese \\( (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\\) tiles are pairwise disjoint, each lies entirely in \\(L\\) or in \\(R\\), and together they cover every square except the diagonal holes. Hence the bound (3) is tight.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Conclusion for \\(n=2025\\)\n\nSubstituting \\(n=2025\\) into the formula \\(2n-2\\) gives\n\n\\[\n2\\cdot2025-2=4048.\n\\]\n\nTherefore the minimum possible number of tiles is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}.\n\\]"
